    "case_id": "97",
    "case_summary": "A 36-year-old, postpartum, white female presented with sharp, low abdominal pain occurring at the end of each urination and visible blood in the urine. She reported no night sweats, fever, or history of urinary tract infection, trauma, instrumentation, or other urological issues. An ultrasound of the kidneys and urinary bladder identified a broad-based polypoid mass measuring 2.4 × 2.3 × 2.2 cm on the right posterolateral wall of the bladder. The patient underwent a transurethral resection of the identified mass.\n\nHistologic examination of the excised mass displayed infiltration of the bladder wall by an atypical spindle cell proliferation with histologic features of myofibroblasts arranged in sheets with focal myxoid areas, whereas the overlying transitional epithelium was normal. The tumor cells demonstrated prominent nucleoli and nuclear pleomorphism; numerous normal mitotic figures (5/10hpf) and a few abnormal ones were seen. By immunohistochemical evaluation, the cells were: (a) positive for vimentin and ALK-1; (b) focally positive for cytokeratins 8/18/19, muscle specific and smooth muscle actins, and desmin; (c) negative for S-100, CD34, c-Kit (CD117), h-caldesmon and MyoD1.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
